    "### Introduction\n",
    "The Global Preferences Survey measures core economic and psychological traits across tens of thousands of people in dozens of countries. This report looks at how different conditions relate to trust around the world, using three hypotheses:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Language diversity:** Countries where more languages are spoken will have lower trust scores. Multilingual countries may face more language barriers, which can make information harder to interpret and trust.\n",
    "2. **Risk taking:** Higher trust is correlated with higher risk taking. People who trust others more may be more willing to take risks.\n",
    "3. **Altruism:** People with higher trust scores will have higher altruism scores than people with lower trust scores.\n",
    "\n",
    "Together, these lead to the research question: **How do structural factors, such as linguistic diversity, and individual traits, such as risk taking and altruism, relate to variations in trust across countries?**"
   ]
  },
